Core Web Development Terms

-HTML — The structure and content of a webpage.
CSS — Controls styling, layout, colors, spacing, typography, etc.
JavaScript — Adds behavior and interactivity to websites.
DOM (Document Object Model) — The browser’s representation of a webpage that JavaScript can interact with.
Responsive Design — Designing a site so it works well across different screen sizes.
Viewport — The visible area of a webpage on a device.
Breakpoint — A screen-width threshold where a layout changes.
Flexbox — A CSS layout system for arranging elements.
Grid — A layout system based on rows and columns.
Semantic HTML — Using meaningful HTML elements such as <header>, <nav>, <main>, and <footer>.
Accessibility (a11y) — Making websites usable by people with disabilities.
SEO — Optimizing a website so it can perform better in search engines.
Meta Tags — Information about a page used by search engines and browsers.
Canonical URL — Identifies the preferred version of a URL to search engines.
Favicon — The small icon associated with a website/page in browser tabs.
Wix-Specific Terms
Wix Editor — Wix's visual website-building environment.
Wix Studio — Wix's professional web development platform for more advanced design and responsive control.
Velo by Wix — Wix's development environment for adding JavaScript-based functionality.
Wix CMS — Wix's content management system for storing and displaying structured content.
CMS Collection — A database-like collection containing structured website content.
Dataset — A Wix element that connects page elements to CMS collection data.
Dynamic Page — A page whose content is generated from CMS data rather than being manually created for each item.
Dynamic URL — A URL generated from CMS data, often containing an item's slug.
Repeater — A Wix element that displays multiple items using the same layout.
Wix App Market — Marketplace for adding apps and functionality to Wix sites.
Wix Dashboard — The administrative area where site owners manage settings, content, analytics, products, etc.
Site Members — Wix functionality for user accounts and member-only areas.
Roles & Permissions — Controls determining who can access or modify certain content.
Wix Forms — Tools for collecting information from website visitors.
Wix Stores — Wix's ecommerce functionality.
Wix Bookings — Tools for appointments, services, classes, and scheduling.
Wix Events — Tools for creating and managing events.
Wix Automations — Rules that trigger actions based on events or conditions.
Wix Analytics — Tools for measuring website traffic and visitor behavior.
Velo / Coding Terms
Frontend — Code and functionality that runs in the visitor's browser.
Backend — Server-side code that runs away from the visitor's browser.
API — A way for different software systems to communicate.
REST API — An API architecture commonly used for exchanging data over HTTP.
Endpoint — A specific URL through which an API provides functionality or data.
HTTP Request — A request sent between a client and server.
GET — HTTP method generally used to retrieve data.
POST — HTTP method generally used to submit or create data.
JSON — A common format for exchanging structured data.
Promise — A JavaScript object representing an operation that may complete in the future.
Async/Await — JavaScript syntax for working with asynchronous operations.
Event Handler — Code that runs in response to something happening, such as a click.
Function — A reusable block of code.
Variable — A named value that can be stored and manipulated.
Array — A JavaScript structure used to store multiple values.
Object — A JavaScript structure containing properties and values.
Scope — Determines where a variable or function can be accessed.
Import / Export — Mechanisms for sharing code between JavaScript modules.
Module — A reusable piece of code that can be imported into another file.
Console — A browser/development tool used to inspect output and errors.
Debugging — Finding and fixing problems in code.
Wix CMS & Data Terms
Collection — A structured set of CMS records.
Field — A specific type of data stored in a collection.
Item / Record — One individual entry in a collection.
Reference Field — A field that connects one CMS collection to another.
Multi-Reference Field — Allows an item to be associated with multiple items from another collection.
Query — A request for specific data from a collection or API.
Filter — Restricts which records are returned.
Sort — Determines the order in which records appear.
Pagination — Splitting a large set of results into smaller pages.
CRUD — Create, Read, Update, Delete — the four basic data operations.
Permissions — Rules controlling who can read, create, update, or delete CMS data.
Data Hook — Velo code that runs automatically during certain CMS data operations.
wixData — Velo API used to work with Wix CMS data.
Website Architecture Terms
Header — The top section of a webpage, commonly containing navigation and branding.
Navigation / Navbar — Links used to move around a website.
Footer — The bottom section of a webpage.
Landing Page — A page designed around a specific marketing or conversion goal.
404 Page — The page shown when a requested URL doesn't exist.
Slug — The readable portion of a URL identifying a specific page/content item.
URL Structure — The organization and format of website URLs.
Sitemap — A file/list describing pages that search engines can discover.
301 Redirect — A permanent redirect from one URL to another.
Breadcrumbs — Navigation showing where a user is within a site's hierarchy.
CTA (Call to Action) — An element encouraging the visitor to take an action.
Conversion — A desired visitor action, such as submitting a form or making a purchase.
Performance Terms
Page Load Time — How long a page takes to load.
Core Web Vitals — Google's metrics for evaluating aspects of real-world page experience.
LCP (Largest Contentful Paint) — Measures how quickly the main content becomes visible.
INP (Interaction to Next Paint) — Measures responsiveness to user interactions.
CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift) — Measures unexpected movement of page content.
Lazy Loading — Loading resources only when they're needed.
Image Optimization — Reducing image size while maintaining acceptable quality.
Caching — Storing resources temporarily so they can be delivered faster.
Minification — Removing unnecessary characters from code to reduce file size.
CDN (Content Delivery Network) — A distributed network that delivers website resources from locations closer to users.
SEO Terms Every Wix Developer Should Know
Keyword — A search term users enter into a search engine.
Search Intent — The reason behind a user's search.
Title Tag — The title associated with a webpage in search results and browser contexts.
Meta Description — A description of a page that may appear in search results.
Heading Hierarchy — Proper use of H1, H2, H3, etc.
Alt Text — Text describing an image, primarily for accessibility and image context.
Internal Link — A link from one page on a website to another page on the same website.
Backlink — A link from another website to your website.
Schema Markup / Structured Data — Machine-readable information that helps search engines understand page content.
Indexing — The process by which search engines store and potentially retrieve webpages.
Crawling — The process search engines use to discover and inspect webpages.
Robots.txt — Instructions that can influence how search-engine crawlers access a site.
Search Console — Google's tool for monitoring a site's presence in Google Search.
Professional Terms Worth Knowing
Wireframe — A basic visual blueprint of a webpage.
UI (User Interface) — The visual elements people interact with.
UX (User Experience) — The overall experience of using a website.
Design System — A reusable set of design rules and components.
Component — A reusable UI element or group of elements.
Template — A reusable starting structure for a website or page.
Client Requirements — The functionality and design requirements provided by a client.
Scope Creep — Additional work that gradually gets added beyond the original project scope.
QA (Quality Assurance) — Testing a website before launch.
Staging Site — A non-public environment used for testing.
Production Site — The live version of a website.
Deployment — Publishing code or changes to a live environment.
Version Control — Tracking changes to code over time.
Git — A widely used version-control system.
Repository (Repo) — A location where code and its history are stored.
Third-Party Integration — Connecting Wix to an external service or platform.
Webhook — A mechanism that allows one service to notify another when an event occurs.
Authentication — Verifying who a user is.
Authorization — Determining what an authenticated user is allowed to do.
